\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



Here sum-free'' forbids writing a member as a sum of any number of distinct smaller members, rather than just two members. Let $d_n=a_{n+1}-a_n$. The source asks how slowly these successive gaps can grow and, in particular, whether the eventual bound $d_n<n$ can be achieved. An isolated small gap is not the intended asymptotic question. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/876}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $A=\{a_1<a_2<\cdots\}\subset \mathbb{N}$ be an infinite sum-free set - that is, there are no solutions to $ a=b_1+\cdots+b_r $ with $b_1<\cdots<b_r<a\in A$. How small can $a_{n+1}-a_n$ be? Is it possible that $a_{n+1}-a_n<n$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Determine the minimal possible gap scale. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can an infinite integer set avoid every representation of one element as a sum of distinct smaller elements while keeping successive gaps below their indices?

